2.5 Known Gaps and Limitations
Data Representation:
* Dimensionality Imbalance: More 8D functions than lower dimensions (reflects challenge of high-dimensional optimization)
* Sparse High-Dimensional Sampling: Initial 20-50 points in 8D space provides limited coverage due to curse of dimensionality
* No Noise: All function evaluations are deterministic (no measurement noise or stochasticity)
Function Properties:
* Continuity: All functions are continuous (no discrete variables)
* Differentiability: Functions are smooth (no corners or discontinuities)
* Bounds: All functions have defined input bounds; no unbounded optimization
* Single-Objective: Only single objective per function (no multi-objective optimization)
* No Constraints: Functions have no equality or inequality constraints
Coverage:
* Initial Sampling: Latin Hypercube Sampling may not uniformly cover 8D space due to curse of dimensionality
* Optimization Trajectory: Only 10 refinement steps per function (limited trajectory data)
* Function Diversity: All functions are synthetic benchmarks (limited real-world complexity)

3. COLLECTION PROCESS
3.1 Data Generation Strategy
Phase 1: Initial Training Data
Method: Latin Hypercube Sampling (LHS)
sampler = qmc.LatinHypercube(d=8)
X_initial = qmc.scale(
    sampler.random(n=N_initial),
    l_bounds=[lower bounds per dimension],
    u_bounds=[upper bounds per dimension]
)
Specifications:
* Sampling Method: Latin Hypercube Sampling ensures space-filling coverage
* Number of Initial Points: 20-50 points per function
* Bounds: Function-specific parameter ranges from literature
* Purpose: Create diverse, well-distributed initial training set
Phase 2: Iterative Bayesian Optimization
Round Structure (10 rounds total, 1 evaluation per round):
1. Fit Gaussian Process Surrogate:
    * Kernel: ConstantKernel(1.0) × Matern(ν=2.5, length_scale=1.0)
    * Regularization: alpha=0.01 (noise tolerance for stability)
    * Optimizer: 30 restarts for hyperparameter optimization
    * Input/Output Scaling: StandardScaler applied to both X and y (critical for GP)
2. Generate Candidate Pool:
    * Method: Latin Hypercube Sampling
    * Size: 30,000 candidate points
    * Purpose: Explore search space broadly
3. Predict on Candidates:
    * Use fitted GP to predict mean (μ) and std (σ) on all candidates
    * Ensure predictions are rescaled to original units
4. Compute Acquisition Function:
    * Function: Upper Confidence Bound (UCB)
    * Formula: UCB = μ + κ × σ
    * Parameter: κ = 2.0 (exploration/exploitation trade-off)
5. Select Next Point:
    * Select point with highest UCB score
    * Ensure point respects function bounds
6. Evaluate and Update:
    * Evaluate selected point on true objective function
    * Add to training dataset
    * Record: X_new, y_new, round number, UCB score
7. Repeat: Iterations 1-6 for 10 rounds total
